  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe southern Adirondacks, Mohawk Valley, and Lake George- \nSaratoga regions are likely to continue to see light drizzle to \ndry conditions through the next one to two hours before light \naccumulating precipitation moves in from the south and brings \nlight rainfall amounts between 0.1 to 0.25 inches. For \neverywhere else across eastern New York and western New England,\nperiods of light to a steady normal rainfall continues through \ntonight. Rainfall totals range between 0.5 to 1.25 inches \nthrough early tomorrow morning. As precipitation has started off\nmore as a showery nature, a more stratiform rainfall is moving \nthrough locations south and east of Albany through tonight. \nRainfall begins to taper off between 2 AM and 4 AM, becoming \nisolated lingering rain showers for locations north of Albany. \nAs mostly dry conditions have occurred the last four to five \ndays across portions of eastern New York and western New England\nand steady light to a normal rainfall is occurring, there are \nno flash flooding impacts expected through this evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAfter a brief break in the precipitation chances Monday, unsettled \nweather conditions return Tuesday as a low pressure system from the \nnorth slides into New York Tuesday morning. This weather system \nbrings additional beneficial rainfall to eastern New York and \nwestern New York with medium (greater than 50%) to high chances \nof rain showers throughout the day. Tuesday late afternoon into \nthe overnight hours, a strong cold front is currently forecasted\nto move through and with enough ingredients, there could be a \nfew rumbles of thunder across locations north of Albany during \nTuesday afternoon ahead of the front. For locations south of \nAlbany, confidence is lower for rumbles of thunder as \ningredients are not strongly favored to materialize at this \nforecast time. Latest probabilistic forecast guidance suggests \nfor locations north of Albany have a greater than 50% chance of \nmore than 0.5 inches of rain in 24 hours from Tuesday morning \nthrough Wednesday morning. This brings increasing confidence for\nrainfall and a dreary Tuesday is favored by the latest \nforecast. \n\nBelow normal temperatures return behind the cold front as a colder \nair mass above the surface returns for Wednesday through the end\nof the week. High temperatures Thursday and Friday could range \nin the 50s and low 60s. Normal high temperatures for the middle\nof October across the Greater Capital Region range between 62 \nand 64 degrees, where Thursday high temperatures could struggle \nto reach above 60. In the Mid-Hudson Valley, highs are \ncurrently forecast to range in the low 60s. Friday night into\nSaturday morning could be our first tap into colder air with\nlow to medium chances for precipitation as low temperatures \ncould range in the 20s in the southern Adirondacks for the first\nsnowflakes of the season to fly.",
